														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4551586">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Thoughts!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Thought.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I hate to be the fly in this article’s ointment, but: while the idea of a retrocausal pistol is mildly interesting, I found the setup to be a little janky.</p>
<p>Four characters with no established connection are introduced between the second paragraph of the conprocs and the first of the description. A little too brisk for me!</p>
<p>I think it would flow better if the Lockley/Clay section were framed as an <em>update</em> in response to the ASCI document. It signals to the reader that there’s a good reason for these conprocs that will be revealed later. But as it stands, having that many names unveiled at once left me a bit breathless.</p>
<p>I also tripped over this -</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5908 was delivered six days after Andrew Jackson's death and remained unused until recovered by the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>because I’ve been conditioned to interpret that as “delivered <em>to the Foundation</em>”. (You hadn’t introduced the warehouse yet!) For that reason, I think Description/Discovery would work better if woven into a single section.</p>
<p>In a nutshell, I think this is an issue of clarity. There’s quite a few things going on in your conprocs and description. None are hard to understand by themselves. But I feel that they don’t gel together harmoniously to set up the backstory in a satisfying way.</p>
<p>It’s a novote from me. Sorry!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

